<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bessarabia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bessarabia is a historical region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> rivers in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the eastern half of the medieval Principality of Moldavia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1812. It united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1918, was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940, and today forms most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Republic of Moldova</a>, with its northern and southern fringes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bessarabia.jpg" alt="Bessarabia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ЯдвигаВереск · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81_%D0%90%D0%BA%D0%BA%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%97_%D1%84%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%86%D1%96,_%D0%91%D1%96%D0%BB%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4-%D0%94%D0%BD%D1%96%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bessarabia is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> to the north and east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> to the west, and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the south, and has an area of 45,630 square kilometres. It is mostly hilly plains and flat steppe, very fertile. About two thirds of it lies in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, with the Budjak in the south and a small part of the Chernivtsi Oblast in the north in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. According to the traditional explanation the name derives from the Wallachian Basarab dynasty, who allegedly ruled the south in the 14th century; some scholars argue that it began as an exonym used by Western cartographers and was first used in local sources only in the late 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In antiquity the region was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> and, for shorter periods, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, Sarmatians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>. Greek settlers founded the colony of Tyras in the 6th century BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> defeated the Dacian kingdom in 106 CE. From the 3rd to the 11th century it was invaded in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. After the 1360s it was gradually included in the Principality of Moldavia, which by 1392 controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Akkerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a>. Stephen the Great ruled from 1457 to 1504 and built churches and monasteries near his battlefields, many along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>. In 1484 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> captured Chilia and Akkerman, and in 1538 more land in the south.</p><p>By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 28 May 1812 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ceded the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the whole of it was then called Bessarabia. German settlers arrived from 1814, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and Gagauz settled in the south. The south returned to Moldavia in 1856, and Russian rule over the whole region was restored in 1878, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> exchanged it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>. On 9 April 1918 its assembly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfatul-tarii">Sfatul Țării</a>, voted for union with Romania; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> never recognised this. In 1940, under an ultimatum, Romania withdrew; it retook the region in 1941 and lost it in 1944, and the Treaty of Paris of 1947 recognised the border along the Prut.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved, the Moldavian and Ukrainian republics declared independence in 1991 and kept the existing division of Bessarabia, so that most of it forms the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> and its northern and southern fringes lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Chișinău, the former capital of the Russian Bessarabia Governorate, is the capital of Moldova.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bessarabia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bessarabia</b>, annexed by Russia in 1812, became in 1991 the core of the independent Republic of Moldova, northern Bukovina and southern Bessarabia belong to Ukraine, and western Moldavia forms the north-east of Romania, where Iași remains a leading university city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact of August 1939 the Soviet Union invaded eastern Poland, fought the costly Winter War against Finland and annexed the Baltic states and <b>Bessarabia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Russia accepted the neutralisation of the Black Sea, barring warships from its waters, and gave up southern <b>Bessarabia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1918 <b>Bessarabia</b>, Bukovina and Transylvania joined, creating Greater Romania.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Russian monarchy in 1917, <b>Bessarabia's</b> Romanian-speaking majority organised politically.</blockquote><a href="/state/moldavian-democratic-republic">Moldavian Democratic Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>It also fought the Kingdom of Romania, which had occupied <b>Bessarabia</b>, and reached a short-lived ceasefire agreement with it.</blockquote><a href="/state/odessa-soviet-republic">Odessa Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
